Widen the nova_scheduler healthcheck in CI.

nova_scheduler's healthcheck is "healthcheck_port nova-scheduler
5672", which asserts an established AMQP connection. Since Nova
33.0.0 (2026.1) every Nova service sleeps for
[DEFAULT]/manager_shutdown_timeout, which is 160 seconds by default,
inside its SIGTERM handler, so every deploy and every reconfigure
leaves the scheduler with no AMQP connection for the best part of
three minutes. Nova's release note for that feature says as much,
and tells operators to widen their supervision timeouts to suit:

  The default value of [DEFAULT]/graceful_shutdown_timeout has been
  changed from 60 to 180 seconds for all Nova services. [...]
  Operators using external system (e.g. k8s, systemd) to manage the
  Nova services should ensure that their service stop timeouts are
  set to at least graceful_shutdown_timeout to avoid forcefully
  killing service before Nova finish its graceful shutdown.

Kolla-Ansible supervises Nova with container healthchecks rather
than systemd, and those were never widened to match. The default 30
second interval with 3 retries tolerates only 90 seconds, so
whether check-failure.sh trips comes down to where its poll lands
in the restart window. From a rocky-10 job on 2026-08-03:

  01:38:55.303 Caught SIGTERM signal, graceful exiting of service
  01:38:56.787 service stopped RPC server on topic: scheduler
  01:38:56.787 Scheduler service manager is waiting for 160 seconds
               to finish in-progress tasks
  01:41:36.787 manager graceful shutdown finished
  01:41:36.850 Starting scheduler node (version 33.1.0)

The post-deploy sanity check ran at 01:40:53, inside that window,
and the job failed. Across a trailing 30 days this shows up in 328
Kolla CI builds and is fatal to 118 of them, across 13 job variants
and spread evenly over rocky-10, ubuntu-noble and debian-trixie.
Every one of those is on master or stable/2026.1 and none is on an
older branch, which is exactly where the Nova change landed.

Every service already accepts per-service healthcheck overrides via
<service>_healthcheck_<option>, so no code change is needed. Set
nova_scheduler_healthcheck_retries to 8 in the CI globals template,
giving 240 seconds and comfortably clearing the restart window.
This follows 795fbb104, which widened
mariadb_monitor_galera_healthcheck_timeout in the same file for the
same class of problem.

Also document the knob in the sample globals.yml, alongside the
defaults it overrides, and add a release note, since operators hit
the same window on any reconfigure. The sample entry stays
commented out and changes nothing for an existing deployment.

Nova describes the feature as experimental and plans to replace the
fixed sleep with real task tracking (blueprint
nova-services-graceful-shutdown-part2-task-tracking, currently in
review as I2de2172b5836f156c2154ca73cbff558ec587ea3). Until that
lands the wait is unconditional, and for nova_scheduler it is dead
time: nova/service.py stops and drains the RPC server before
calling the manager's graceful_shutdown(), and only nova-compute
creates the second RPC server that can accept work during
shutdown. This override should be dropped once the Nova side is
fixed. nova_conductor and nova_compute share the same probe shape
and the same sleep, so they can trip the same way; only the
scheduler has been observed doing so in CI.

Prompt: Michael asked for assistance debugging why CI jobs were
failing with nova-scheduler timeouts. We then traced the delay to
Nova's manager_shutdown_timeout, and confirming 2026.1 shipped that
behaviour, produced the citations above.

